Branch: `feature/SFCC-236_product-card-shared-component` (confirm via `git branch --show-current` before editing). This is a new, standalone bug fix, not part of the existing plan file at /Users/marcel.a.n.navarro/.claude/plans/twinkly-swinging-umbrella.md — found by the developer live-testing a related fix ("Fix I") that just made touch-swipe paging work on `ZimmProductCard`'s image gallery. Do not touch anything related to Fix I or the separate, currently-in-progress hover-reveal accessibility fix ("Fix J") — those are other agents'/steps' scope. This step only touches the slide-indicator update mechanism.

## The bug

`overrides/app/components/ui-kits/molecules/zimmProductCardMedia/index.tsx`, the `useEffect` at line 211 (comment starting line 205) only updates the touch slide-indicator's active dot via a `scrollend` listener:

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
  const container = scrollContainerRef.current;
  if (!container || totalSlides <= 1) return undefined;

  const handleScrollEnd = () => {
    const slideWidth = container.clientWidth || 1;
    const nearest = Math.round(container.scrollLeft / slideWidth);
    setCurrentIndex(Math.min(Math.max(nearest, 0), totalSlides - 1));
  };

  container.addEventListener('scrollend', handleScrollEnd);
  return () => container.removeEventListener('scrollend', handleScrollEnd);
}, [totalSlides]);
```

`scrollend` fires only once a scroll/snap gesture has fully *settled* (after the finger lifts and any momentum/snap animation finishes) — it never fires *during* an active drag. So the indicator dot stays on the old slide the whole time the user's finger is moving, then jumps to the correct one only once the gesture ends — this reads as "laggy," confirmed live by the developer after a related fix made the swipe gesture itself reachable for the first time.

**The existing comment above this effect (lines 205-210) is itself slightly wrong and needs correcting as part of this fix**, not just the code: it frames the lag as a graceful-degradation fallback specifically for browsers *without* `scrollend` support ("the indicator dot simply lags on an unsupported browser"). That's not the real tradeoff — even on a browser with full `scrollend` support, tracking via `scrollend` alone can never be live during an active drag, because the event doesn't fire until the gesture is over, regardless of support. Fix the comment's reasoning, don't just patch the code.

## The fix

Add a `scroll` listener alongside the existing `scrollend` one, coalesced via `requestAnimationFrame` so it updates at most once per animation frame (avoiding excessive `setState` calls — native touch-driven scrolling fires many `scroll` events per second, and `scrollLeft` genuinely does update live during an active drag, unlike `scrollend`). Keep `scrollend` too, as a final-correctness safety net (in case the last in-frame scroll position during a fast gesture doesn't get its own rAF-coalesced update before the listener is asked to fire again). Concretely, replace the effect with something equivalent to:

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
  const container = scrollContainerRef.current;
  if (!container || totalSlides <= 1) return undefined;

  const updateIndexFromScrollPosition = () => {
    const slideWidth = container.clientWidth || 1;
    const nearest = Math.round(container.scrollLeft / slideWidth);
    setCurrentIndex(Math.min(Math.max(nearest, 0), totalSlides - 1));
  };

  let rafId: number | null = null;
  const handleScroll = () => {
    if (rafId !== null) return;
    rafId = requestAnimationFrame(() => {
      rafId = null;
      updateIndexFromScrollPosition();
    });
  };

  container.addEventListener('scroll', handleScroll, { passive: true });
  container.addEventListener('scrollend', updateIndexFromScrollPosition);
  return () => {
    container.removeEventListener('scroll', handleScroll);
    container.removeEventListener('scrollend', updateIndexFromScrollPosition);
    if (rafId !== null) cancelAnimationFrame(rafId);
  };
}, [totalSlides]);
```

Use your own judgement on exact naming/structure (e.g. whether to keep one shared inner function or two) as long as the behavior is: `scroll` events drive live, rAF-throttled updates during an active drag; `scrollend` remains as an unconditional final correction; the cleanup function removes both listeners and cancels any pending rAF. `{ passive: true }` on the `scroll` listener is intentional (it never calls `preventDefault`) — keep it.

Update the comment block above the effect (currently lines 205-210) to correctly describe the new behavior and the real reasoning above (not "lags on an unsupported browser" — say plainly that `scrollend`-only tracking can't be live on any browser, which is why `scroll` is now also used, throttled via `requestAnimationFrame` for performance).

## Test updates

`overrides/app/components/ui-kits/molecules/zimmProductCardMedia/index.test.tsx` already has a test exercising the `scrollend`-driven indicator update via a synthetic `Event('scrollend')` (search for it — likely sets `container.scrollLeft`/mocks `clientWidth` then dispatches the event and asserts the indicator's active index). Read it in full first. Add an equivalent test for the new `scroll`-driven path: dispatch a synthetic `Event('scroll')` after setting `scrollLeft`, and assert the index updates — you'll need to actually flush the `requestAnimationFrame` callback in the test for the assertion to see the update (check whether this project's Vitest setup already mocks/shims `requestAnimationFrame`, e.g. via `vitest.setup.ts` or jsdom defaults, and use whatever mechanism is already established in this codebase for flushing rAF in a test — such as `vi.useFakeTimers({toFake: ['requestAnimationFrame']})` + `vi.advanceTimersToNextFrame()`/`vi.runAllTimers()`, or a manual `requestAnimationFrame` mock if that's what's already set up elsewhere in this test suite; grep the repo for any existing rAF-flushing precedent before inventing one). Also add/confirm a test that a `scroll` event fired in rapid succession only results in one state update per animation frame (not one per event) — this is the actual behavior being added, not just "it updates eventually."

## Scope

Only touch `zimmProductCardMedia/index.tsx` and `zimmProductCardMedia/index.test.tsx`. Do not touch `zimmProductCard.css.ts`, `zimmProductCardMedia.css.ts`, `zimmProductCard/index.tsx`, or `zimmProductCard/index.test.tsx` — all owned by other in-flight work right now. Run `npx tsc --noEmit` and this project's lint command before reporting done, and paste the actual output, plus the full Vitest run for this component's test file. Report back exactly what you changed and why the rAF-throttling approach is safe (won't miss the final settled position, won't over-fire state updates).

